docker volume
-------------
Volume    = persistent data  Persistent data = data that should remain even after the container is deleted.
Container = temporary
“Docker volume is a storage mechanism used to persist data outside the container. Normally, when a container is deleted, the data inside the container can be lost.
So we use a volume to store important data separately. The volume can be attached to one or more containers, and even if the container is deleted,
the data in the volume remains.”

“Docker volume is used for persistent storage. It keeps data independent of the container, so the data remains even when the container is deleted.”
Why use Docker volumes?
Persistence: Data remains even if the container is deleted.
Data sharing: Multiple containers can use the same volume.
Performance: Generally faster and more reliable than bind mounts for Docker-managed data.
Portability: Docker manages the storage location, making it easier to migrate.

1. Named Volume
Docker manages the storage location.
docker volume create mydata
docker run -v mydata:/app/data ubuntu
Interview: “A named volume is managed by Docker and is mainly used for persistent application data.”

2.Bind Mount               here we not created a volume so we use file or fol;der to store a data
You specify a directory from the host machine.
docker run -v /home/user/data:/app/data ubuntu
Here:
Host                         Container
/home/user/data   ───────→   /app/data
/home/user/data = folder on your host machine
/app/data = folder inside the container
This is called a bind mount
Data written to /app/data is actually stored in /home/user/data on the host.
Interview: “A bind mount maps a specific file or directory from the host to the container.”

3.Anonymous Volume
Docker creates the volume automatically without you giving it a name.
docker run -v /app/data ubuntu
Docker generates a random volume name.
Interview: “An anonymous volume is automatically created by Docker and doesn't have a user-defined name.”

