  tar czf /backup/backup.tar.gz /data  You don't need to create another permanent container. 
This command creates a temporary Ubuntu container, performs the backup, and removes it.

Suppose on your host: /home/sunil/ is your current directory.
When we run: -v $(pwd):/backup
Docker connects them:
HOST                         CONTAINER
/home/sunil  ─────────────→   /backup
Initially:/backup
          └── (empty)
Then this command: tar czf /backup/backup.tar.gz /data
means: Take everything from /data and create backup.tar.gz inside /backup.
So after tar runs: Temporary Container
/data
└── file1.txt
└── file2.txt
/backup
└── backup.tar.gz   ← CREATED by tar
Because /backup is connected to the host:
Container                    Host
/backup
└── backup.tar.gz  ───────→  /home/siva/backup.tar.gz
So the key point
/backup does NOT need to already contain data.
It is simply the destination where we create the backup file.

Interview answer
“To back up a Docker volume, I mount the volume into a temporary container, use tar to compress its contents,
and mount a host directory to save the resulting backup file.”


We already have: backup.tar.gz
This file contains the data that was previously inside our volume.
Now suppose the original volume is empty or we created a new volume. We want to put the backup data back into it.
Step 1 — Create a new volume
docker volume create my_volume
Now:
my_volume
   ↓
(empty)
Step 2 — Run a temporary container
docker run --rm \
  -v my_volume:/data \
  -v $(pwd):/backup \
  ubuntu \
  tar xzf /backup/backup.tar.gz -C /

Again, we have two mounts.
First:
-v my_volume:/data
This connects the empty volume to the container:
my_volume ─────→ /data

This is the destination for our restored data.
Second:
-v $(pwd):/backup
This connects the host's current directory to /backup:
Host folder ─────→ /backup
Our backup file is there:
Host
└── backup.tar.gz
Inside the container:
/backup
└── backup.tar.gz
Step 3 — Extract the backup
This part:
tar xzf /backup/backup.tar.gz -C /       -C / means extract the files starting from the root directory /.
means: Take backup.tar.gz from /backup and extract its contents starting at /.
The backup contains something like:
data/
└── file.txt
So extraction puts it back into:
/data/
└── file.txt
And /data is connected to my_volume.
Therefore:
backup.tar.gz
      ↓
   extract
      ↓
   /data
      ↓
 my_volume
Why --rm again?

The container is only being used to perform the restore.

After extraction:

Restore complete
      ↓
Temporary container removed

But:

my_volume       → remains
restored data   → remains
backup.tar.gz   → remains



