depends_on 
--------------
 means  “Start this service after the service I depend on has been started.”
Your startup order
Because of depends_on, Compose starts them in this order:
database
   ↓
backend
   ↓
frontend

when you run :  docker compose up -d
Compose understands:
database must be started first
        ↓
backend can be started
        ↓
frontend can be started

Important point ⚠️
depends_on does NOT mean the application is fully ready.
For example:

database container starts
        ↓
MySQL is still initializing
        ↓
backend container starts

the database container has started, but MySQL might still be preparing the database.
So depends_on mainly controls service startup order, not "wait until the service is completely ready."
For true readiness, you can use a healthcheck with condition: service_healthy.

why we starting first what is the udse of
We start backend before frontend because the frontend may need the backend to process requests.
Think of it like this:
User
  ↓
Frontend
  ↓
Backend
  ↓
Database
Why backend first?
Suppose the frontend has a Login button.

When the user clicks Login:
Frontend
   ↓
"Check username/password"
   ↓
Backend
   ↓
Database

The frontend sends the request to the backend.
If the backend is not running:
Frontend → Backend ❌
The request fails.
So depends_on tells Docker Compose:
frontend:
  depends_on:
    - backend

Meaning: Start the backend service before starting the frontend service.

This tells Compose that the database service should be started before the backend service.
But important: depends_on does not automatically mean the database is fully ready to accept connections.
