health check
----------
We use healthcheck to check whether a container's application is actually ready and working, not just whether the container has started.

services:
  database:
    image: mysql
    healthcheck:
      test: ["CMD", "mysqladmin", "ping", "-h", "localhost"]
      interval: 10s
      timeout: 5s
      retries: 5
Now Compose can check:
MySQL
  ↓
Healthy / Unhealthy

Why do we need it?
When MySQL container starts:
Container starts
      ↓
MySQL is initializing
      ↓
MySQL becomes ready

The container can be Running while MySQL is still initializing.
So:  Running ≠ Ready
The healthcheck checks:
Is MySQL actually responding?
       ↓
      Yes
       ↓
   Healthy

If it isn't responding:
No
 ↓
Unhealthy

depends_on vs healthcheck
backend:
  depends_on:
    - database

means:  Start the database service before starting backend.
But it doesn't necessarily mean:
Wait until MySQL is ready.

healthcheck checks the actual readiness.
Simple example
database container
       ↓
    Starting
       ↓
 healthcheck
       ↓
Is MySQL responding?
    ↙       ↘
  No         Yes
  ↓           ↓
Unhealthy   Healthy
              ↓
        Backend can connect

In short:
depends_on → startup dependency
healthcheck → is the application actually ready/healthy?

test: ["CMD", "mysqladmin", "ping", "-h", "localhost"]
This is the command Docker runs inside the MySQL container to check whether MySQL is working.

Break it down:
CMD
 ↓
Run a command

mysqladmin
 ↓
MySQL administration tool

ping
 ↓
Ask MySQL: "Are you responding?"

-h
 ↓
Specify the MySQL host

localhost
 ↓
The MySQL server inside this same container

So essentially:
Docker
  ↓
Runs mysqladmin ping -h localhost
  ↓
MySQL responds?
  ↓
YES → Healthy
NO  → Unhealthy

interval: 10s
Means: Run the health check every 10 seconds.
10 sec → check
10 sec → check
10 sec → check
...

3. timeout: 5s
timeout: 5s
Means: Give the health-check command up to 5 seconds to respond.

If it doesn't respond within 5 seconds:
Check
 ↓
5 seconds passed
 ↓
Failed

4. retries: 5
retries: 5
Means: If the health check keeps failing, allow 5 consecutive failures before Docker marks the container as unhealthy.
For example:
Check 1 → ❌
Check 2 → ❌
Check 3 → ❌
Check 4 → ❌
Check 5 → ❌
             ↓
         Unhealthy
